# Reference decodes stored beside the files they belong to. A PPM is a short
# ASCII header in front of raw samples, and one whose samples happen to contain
# no zero byte reads as text, so a checkout that converts line endings rewrites
# the picture. Naming them binary is what stops that: the flat-colour JPEG XL
# fixture reached Windows CI three bytes out of step and failed there alone.
*.ppm binary
*.pgm binary
*.pbm binary
*.pnm binary
*.pam binary

# The PE resource oracle: a DLL rc.exe and link.exe built, and the cursor and icon it was built
# from. Their whole value is that the bytes are Microsoft's rather than ours, so nothing may be
# allowed to rewrite them on the way through a checkout. Git's own heuristic would almost
# certainly call all three binary -- each begins with a NUL -- but almost is not the standard a
# fixture used as evidence should be held to.
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.cur binary
*.ico binary
